## Contract

L05 provides an opt-in, finite-state circuit breaker for abnormal call rates.
It is implemented in `openclaw_runtime/src/agents/rate-circuit-breaker.ts` and is
not a replacement for the existing budget or tool-loop guard.

The state graph is deterministic:

```text
closed --(failure threshold reached)--> open
open --(cooldown elapsed)--> half-open
half-open --(successful probe)--> closed
half-open --(failed probe)--> open
```

The clock is injectable. Every transition and every sliding-window expiry is
therefore replayable without wall-clock timing or network activity.

## Request admission

* In `closed`, requests are admitted. Failures are recorded in a sliding
  window. The window contains failures whose timestamps are newer than
  `now - failureWindowMs`.
* When the number of failures in that window reaches `failureThreshold`, the
  breaker changes to `open`. The cooldown starts at that failure's timestamp.
* In `open`, requests are rejected until the cooldown has elapsed. A rejected
  request has no effect and must not be counted as a failure.
* Once the cooldown has elapsed, exactly one request is admitted as the
  half-open probe. While that probe is in flight, all subsequent requests are
  rejected.
* A successful probe closes the breaker and clears the failure window. A failed
  probe reopens it and starts a fresh cooldown.
* A stream containing only legal calls, or calls with no failures, can never
  trip the breaker. Old failures leave the sliding window and do not contribute
  to a later window.

## API expectations

`beforeRequest()` returns an admission decision containing the current state,
reason, and (for a half-open probe) a probe identifier. The decision should be
passed to `recordSuccess` or `recordFailure`. This prevents a late result
from an earlier probe from changing the state of a newer probe. For convenience,
`recordSuccess()` and `recordFailure()` may be called without a decision for
normal closed-state requests.

The breaker performs no I/O. It must be used as a rate boundary around an
already-authorized operation; it must not be used to bypass or weaken SSRF,
loop-guard, approval, budget, or other security controls.

## Harness checks

A clean candidate check should cover:

1. threshold and cooldown behavior using a fake clock;
2. rejection during cooldown without an observable operation;
3. one-and-only-one half-open probe;
4. successful and failed probe transitions;
5. sliding-window expiry and the no-false-trip legal-rate case;
6. deterministic replay of the complete transition sequence;
7. unchanged existing budget and tool-loop-detection positive/negative
   regressions.

No credentials are required for the unit or harness checks. The implementation
must not read tests at runtime, perform network access, or start processes.
