## Purpose

The rate circuit breaker limits the effect of an abnormal call rate while
remaining a deliberately narrow boundary. It is opt-in: callers that do not
configure it retain their existing behavior.

The implementation is in
`openclaw_runtime/src/agents/rate-circuit-breaker.ts`.

## State machine

| State | Admission | Success | Failure |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `closed` | Allow | Keep the sliding failure window | Add a failure; open at the threshold |
| `open` | Reject during cooldown | No effect | No effect |
| `half-open` | Allow one probe only | Return to `closed` | Return to `open` and reset cooldown |

The clock is injected through `now`. A fixed or scripted clock makes tests,
harness runs, and replays deterministic. The breaker has no timer, I/O,
process, or network side effects.

## Sliding window semantics

Closed-state failures are retained only while their timestamp is within the
configured failure window. Expiry is evaluated using the injected clock. A
successful normal request does not erase historical failures; the breaker is a
rate detector, not a consecutive-error detector. Consequently, a workload with
no failures never trips it, and failures that have aged out cannot trip a later
legal window.

When the threshold is reached, the breaker enters `open` and starts its
cooldown immediately. Calls made during cooldown are denied and have no
observable effect. At cooldown expiry the breaker becomes `half-open`; the first
request reserves the single probe and concurrent or subsequent requests are
denied until the probe reports its result.

## Integration rules

The breaker does not make an operation safe. It must be placed alongside, or
after, existing authorization and safety boundaries. It does not replace or
relax:

* SSRF protections;
* tool-loop detection and loop guards;
* approval requirements;
* budgets or other existing rate/loop controls.

In particular, the existing budget and tool-loop-detection positive and
negative regressions remain required. A caller must only record an outcome for
an admitted request. Denied requests are not failures and must not be passed
to `recordFailure`.

## Example

```ts
const breaker = createRateCircuitBreaker({
  failureThreshold: 5,
  failureWindowMs: 60_000,
  cooldownMs: 30_000,
  now: () => clock.now,\ // an injected, deterministic clock
});

const decision = breaker.beforeRequest();
if (!decision.allowed) {
  return; // no operation is attempted
}

try {
  await performAuthorizedOperation();
  breaker.recordSuccess(decision);
} catch (error) {
  breaker.recordFailure(decision);
  throw error;
}
```

The probe identifier returned in the decision should be retained with the
request so that a late result cannot be applied to a different half-open probe.
"}]}

